We have seen that we can add $p + p + p$ to get $3p$.

These three terms are called **like terms** because they all contain the same variable, $p$.

We can also add $3x + 4x$. Because both terms have the variable $x$, we can combine the terms using the operation that is given, which in this case is addition. The correct answer is:

$$
\begin{array}{l}
{3x + 4x = 7x}
\end{array}
$$

We can also subtract like terms. We can simplify the expression $10a - 2a$ using subtraction. The correct answer is:

$$
\begin{array}{l}
{10a - 2a = 8a}
\end{array}
$$

When we collect or combine the like terms in this way, we are giving an expression in its simplest form, which is called **simplifying an expression**.
